third parties, and Atlassian awarding me triple their maximum bounty. Out of all that mess, I've managed to extract some

38
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:15,140
information that I hope you'll find really quite useful regardless of whether you've watched Emil's

39
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:21,140
presentation or not. I'm not going to be explaining this in chronological order because looking back on it,

40
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:28,140
it doesn't really make sense even to me. Instead, I'm going to first focus on how to use HTTP 2 for

41
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:36,140
request modeling. Then I'll talk about request tunneling and show how to confirm and exploit this underrated type of vulnerability.

42
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:47,140
Then I'll share four or five new HTTP 2 exploit primitives, share some practical considerations and pitfalls and talk about tooling and effects.

43
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:54,140
After that, I'll answer some questions. Also, if you have any other questions, just tag me on Twitter live and I'll try and answer it there.

44
00:04:54,140 --> 00:05:03,140
Although H2 is complex, there's only actually four key things that you need to understand in order to effectively wield it as a weapon.

45
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:10,140
Here you can see an identical request represented as a H1 request and as a H2 request.

46
00:05:10,140 --> 00:05:17,140
The first key difference is pretty obvious. You can see that H1 has the request line containing the method and the path,

47
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:28,140
whereas H2 has done away with this concept. Instead, they have pseudo headers, which are represented just like normal headers, but the name of them always begins with a colon.

48
00:05:28,140 --> 00:05:40,140
And there's just a few of these predefined in the H2 specification. You can see they use the colon method pseudo header to define the method and colon path to tell the server the path.

49
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:45,140
And there's also colon authority, which is kind of a replacement for the host head.

50
00:05:45,140 --> 00:05:55,140
The second key difference is that H1 is a plain text protocol, which means that server parsing is done with string operations.

51
00:05:55,140 --> 00:06:01,140
For example, server needs to look for new lines to know when one header ends and the next header starts.

52
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:07,140
H2 meanwhile is a binary protocol mostly built using key value pairs.

53
00:06:07,140 --> 00:06:12,140
So what I'm showing you when I represent H2 on any of these slides is an abstraction.

54
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:20,140
It's not what it looks like on the wire. For example, pseudo headers don't actually start with a colon for real.

55
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:28,140
The name colon method is just the human readable name and it's actually mapped to a byte like one.

56
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:40,140
The third key difference is that H1 relies on the user sending a content length or a transfer encoding chunked header in order to tell the server what the length of the messages.

57
00:06:40,140 --> 00:06:49,140
H2 meanwhile doesn't require either of these headers. Instead, the length is built in to the frame layer.

58
00:06:49,140 --> 00:06:53,140
So it's at a slightly lower level that isn't represented by any headers.

59
00:06:53,140 --> 00:07:03,140
And also as a result, it's more or less impossible as far as I know to send a H2 message that is ambiguous about what its length is.

60
00:07:03,140 --> 00:07:10,140
The final key difference is the way they handle multiple requests.

61
00:07:10,140 --> 00:07:20,140
With H1, you send your your send your request down the socket, you read the response and then you just send your next request down the same socket basically by concatenating on the end.

62
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:24,140
And then you read the response straight back down the same socket.

63
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:31,140
And so you're completely relying on the server sending you the responses in the order that you sent the requests.

64
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:34,140
Otherwise, you wouldn't be able to effectively match them up.

65
00:07:34,140 --> 00:07:41,140
H2 makes things a bit more flexible by introducing this concept of streams.

66
00:07:41,140 --> 00:07:51,140
A stream is simply a request response pair and every frame, which is the underlying data type of H2, contains a stream ID.

67
00:07:51,140 --> 00:08:13,140
So the so the browser can just use the stream ID to know which request it should be attaching a response to, which means the responses don't need to come back in the order that the requests were sent in and generally makes things flexible and faster and less likely to go disastrously wrong in the way that we're going to see shortly.

68
00:08:13,140 --> 00:08:16,140
And yeah, that's pretty much it.

69
00:08:16,140 --> 00:08:22,140
So now we understand H2, let's take a look at what damage we can do.

70
00:08:22,140 --> 00:08:33,140
As usual, in this section, I've targeted a bunch of real websites and all these vulnerabilities were found using an automated open source tool that I'm releasing later on.

71
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:40,140
And of the bounties earned, half have been donated to local charities and the other half will be spent on beer when that's allowed.

72
00:08:40,140 --> 00:08:47,140
Why is HP2 request muggling possible?

73
00:08:47,140 --> 00:08:59,140
Because unfortunately, the majority of servers that speak HP2 to the client actually rewrite requests as H1 in order to talk to the backend server.

74
00:08:59,140 --> 00:09:10,140
This behavior is ridiculously common. For example, with Amazon's application load balancer, it's enabled whenever you enable H2 and it can't be disabled.

75
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:15,140
You can't make Amazon's application load balancer speak H2 to the backend server.

76
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:27,140
What this approach, which I'm going to refer to as H2 downgrading does, is it effectively dodges all the security benefits that you should be getting from using H2.

77
00:09:27,140 --> 00:09:31,140
In fact, it arguably makes things worse.

78
00:09:31,140 --> 00:09:42,140
Classic desynchronization using H1 happens because the front end and the backend disagree about whether to use the content length or the transfer and coding header to know the length of the message.

79
00:09:42,140 --> 00:09:51,140
But when you're doing H2 downgrading, it's not possible to have true agreement because the two servers are speaking different protocols.

80
00:09:51,140 --> 00:09:56,140
The front end server is basically guaranteed to be using the H2 to frame length.

81
00:09:56,140 --> 00:09:59,140
That's fine. But then the backend isn't speaking H2.

82
00:09:59,140 --> 00:10:04,140
So it has to use the content length header or the transfer encoding header.

83
00:10:04,140 --> 00:10:09,140
If that sounds like a mess, that's because it absolutely is.

84
00:10:09,140 --> 00:10:19,140
If you take your front end server and turn on H2 support and do downgrading, congratulations, you've just doubled the number of ways that you may be vulnerable to request smuggling.

85
00:10:19,140 --> 00:10:23,140
Let's have a look at what goes wrong.

86
00:10:23,140 --> 00:10:28,140
We're going to start with an extremely simple case study targeting Netflix.

87
00:10:28,140 --> 00:10:42,140
The H2 RFC says that although the content length header is not required in H2 because we already have this frame length, you're actually allowed to send content length header provided it's correct.

88
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:44,140
What could possibly go wrong with that?

89
00:10:44,140 --> 00:10:56,140
Well, Netflix was using the netty Java library for their H2 support and that library forgot to verify that the content length was correct.

90
00:10:56,140 --> 00:11:08,140
So if we send a request like the one you can see here, it would get downgraded to H2 and H1 and forwarded it on with the incorrect content length that you can see on the right.

91
00:11:08,140 --> 00:11:15,140
This would result in the data shown in orange being treated as a separate additional request by the backend server.

92
00:11:15,140 --> 00:11:27,140
And then when the next user's request was sent, that would complete the second fake request and result in the victim user being redirected to my own website.

93
00:11:27,140 --> 00:11:37,140
This could be used to redirect attempts to load JavaScript files, giving persistent JavaScript execution on Netflix's core website.

94
00:11:37,140 --> 00:11:49,140
And just by running this in a loop, I could hijack people's accounts in real time with no user interactions, potentially stealing passwords, credit card numbers and the like.

95
00:11:49,140 --> 00:11:52,140
For that, I got a 20K bounty off Netflix.

96
00:11:52,140 --> 00:12:00,140
After that motivating stuff, let's move on to something slightly more complex.

97
00:12:00,140 --> 00:12:08,140
The H2 RFC says that any message containing connection specific header fields must be cheated as malformed.

98
00:12:08,140 --> 00:12:13,140
But it's a little bit vague about what happens if you don't do this.

99
00:12:13,140 --> 00:12:21,140
So let's find out quite a few major well known servers failed to obey this line of the specification.

100
00:12:21,140 --> 00:12:26,140
And one such server was Amazon's application load balancer.

101
00:12:26,140 --> 00:12:37,140
As a result of this, I could get request smuggling using H2 on pretty much every single site that used Amazon's application load balancer and hadn't disabled HTTP 2.

102
00:12:37,140 --> 00:12:39,140
So let's take a look at this in detail.

103
00:12:39,140 --> 00:12:45,140
So for this example, I'm targeting Oath's law enforcement portal.

104
00:12:45,140 --> 00:12:52,140
And here you can see I'm sending a request that contains the transfer encoding chunked connection specific header field.

105
00:12:52,140 --> 00:12:54,140
So it should be rejected, but it hasn't.

106
00:12:54,140 --> 00:12:57,140
It's been incorrectly forwarded onto the backend.

107
00:12:57,140 --> 00:13:07,140
And this has resulted in the backend giving priority to the transfer encoding chunked header over the content length, which is the one that's actually correct in this case.

108
00:13:07,140 --> 00:13:13,140
Letting me once again prepend content to the start of the next request to hit the server.

109
00:13:13,140 --> 00:13:21,140
When I reported this to Oath, they wanted to see me prove that this was a serious vulnerability.

110
00:13:21,140 --> 00:13:22,140
They weren't entirely convinced.

111
00:13:22,140 --> 00:13:27,140
So I just started running this attack in real time and seeing who I redirected.

112
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:34,140
And what I found was quite a few people that got redirected were in the middle of an OWARTH login flow.

113
00:13:34,140 --> 00:13:44,140
And so they ended up leaking their OWARTH secret codes to me via the referral header, giving me persistent access to their account.

114
00:13:44,140 --> 00:13:49,140
As well as working on Amazon's application load balancer and various other targets.

115
00:13:49,140 --> 00:13:59,140
This also worked on the encapsular web application firewall, which is always good because that file was meant to make your website more secure, not less.

116
00:13:59,140 --> 00:14:03,140
For that, I got a $7,000 bounty off Oath.

117
00:14:03,140 --> 00:14:22,140
I reported this to both encapsular and Amazon, and they both fixed it, but neither of them awarded me with any bounty showing a kind of interesting split between who it is who causes these vulnerabilities and who it is who actually bothers to incentivize people to have the vulnerabilities discovered and fixed.

118
00:14:22,140 --> 00:14:32,140
I actually found another target using the same technique also Amazon's application load balancer and figured, you know, why not exploit these guys too.

119
00:14:32,140 --> 00:14:41,140
So this was a system controlling a CMS owned by AOL that was used for various news websites, I believe.

120
00:14:41,140 --> 00:14:48,140
And once again, I could trigger a redirect and once again, they didn't believe me that this was a very serious security issue.

121
00:14:48,140 --> 00:14:52,140
So I had to put some time into proving the impact.

122
00:14:52,140 --> 00:15:03,140
I just started triggering redirects from people and I found quite a few requests hitting my server that said, hey there, I'd like to have permission to send you my credentials.

123
00:15:03,140 --> 00:15:09,140
And I wasn't expecting that. So after that, nothing really happened until I set my server up.

124
00:15:09,140 --> 00:15:15,140
So it said, yeah, sure, absolutely. Send me your credits. And then sure enough, they did.

125
00:15:15,140 --> 00:15:26,140
I've got a brilliant video taken with TCP dump that shows a fantastic stream of different credentials from live users arriving on my server in real time.

126
00:15:26,140 --> 00:15:32,140
But unfortunately it proved near impossible to redact. So I can't share it publicly.

127
00:15:32,140 --> 00:15:37,140
So for that, I got another 10k off AOL.

128
00:15:37,140 --> 00:15:41,140
That was the basic stuff. Now things are going to get a bit more interesting.

129
00:15:41,140 --> 00:15:49,140
One cool thing about H2 is that being a binary protocol, it lets you put arbitrary characters wherever you like.

130
00:15:49,140 --> 00:15:57,140
And it relies on additional server logic to say things like, no, actually you really shouldn't be putting that character in that position.

131
00:15:57,140 --> 00:16:12,140
Firefox is start page at start.mozilla.org was powered by the Netlify CDN and Netlify forgot to say that you can't put new lines in HTTP headers.

132
00:16:12,140 --> 00:16:31,140
This led to a request header injection vulnerability, which you can see me using here to smuggle a transfer encoding header, thereby causing the front end and the back end to disagree about the length of the message and letting me apply a malicious prefix to requests that hit the server after my payload does.

133
00:16:31,140 --> 00:16:54,140
In this example here, because Netlify is a CDN, I've taken advantage of that by registering my own malicious site with Netlify and I've just changed the host header that I'm sending there so that the second request, although Netlify think it's being sent to Mozilla's start page, is actually going to my website and serving up arbitrary content that I control.

134
00:16:54,140 --> 00:16:59,140
The really cool thing here is that Netlify also comes with a built in cache.

135
00:16:59,140 --> 00:17:11,140
So once this is served up once, it will get saved and effectively using this technique, I get a persistent control over every page over every on every site using the Netlify CDN.

136
00:17:11,140 --> 00:17:18,140
I reported this issue to both Netlify and Mozilla and Got2k off both.

137
00:17:18,140 --> 00:17:27,140
When I tried the same technique on Atlassian's Jira, something really quite interesting and unexpected happened.

138
00:17:27,140 --> 00:17:34,140
I expected to see two different responses coming back to me, the normal response and the poison response.

139
00:17:34,140 --> 00:17:47,140
But what I actually saw, which is you probably can't see because the text is too small, but you'll get the gist, is a huge range of responses that were clearly coming from different Jira deployments,

140
00:17:47,140 --> 00:17:54,140
clearly intended for different people and contained a huge variety of different bits of sensitive information.

141
00:17:54,140 --> 00:18:02,140
This left me wondering what exactly had happened. And eventually I managed to figure it out.

142
00:18:02,140 --> 00:18:11,140
My mistake was I had realised that with this ability to put new lines in headers, I didn't actually need to send a body.

143
00:18:11,140 --> 00:18:18,140
I didn't need to send the transfer encoding chunks header. Instead, I could use multiple new lines to split the request in the header

144
00:18:18,140 --> 00:18:24,140
and then put the additional malicious prefix inside the header.

145
00:18:24,140 --> 00:18:30,140
In doing this, I thought that I'd found a really smart way of sending my normal 1.5 requests.

146
00:18:30,140 --> 00:18:37,140
That meant that I didn't have to use the post method and could therefore potentially exploit a bunch of extra targets.

147
00:18:37,140 --> 00:18:43,140
What I completely failed to account for was that the front end, when it does a downgrade,

148
00:18:43,140 --> 00:18:50,140
is naturally going to terminate the HTTP headers with a couple of new lines.

149
00:18:50,140 --> 00:18:59,140
So I thought I was sending 1.5 requests, but actually I was sending exactly two requests to the back end.

150
00:18:59,140 --> 00:19:04,140
And that meant that I got the response to the first request as usual.

151
00:19:04,140 --> 00:19:07,140
The next user got the second response, which is fine.

152
00:19:07,140 --> 00:19:13,140
But then the next user got the response intended for that user and so on.

153
00:19:13,140 --> 00:19:22,140
Basically, you send one request like this and the front end persistently lost track of which responses it should be sending to which people.

154
00:19:22,140 --> 00:19:26,140
It ends up sending random responses to random users.

155
00:19:26,140 --> 00:19:30,140
And thanks to some of these responses having a set cookie header,

156
00:19:30,140 --> 00:19:36,140
it ended up persistently logging random users into random accounts on Jira.

157
00:19:36,140 --> 00:19:44,140
So that's why Atlassian had to log everyone out and expire everyone's sessions worldwide to just just to mitigate that.

158
00:19:45,140 --> 00:19:52,140
So if you find some request smuggling and someone demands to see real impact and you're struggling to prove it,

159
00:19:52,140 --> 00:19:57,140
then smuggling exactly two requests should get them the evidence that they're looking for.

160
00:19:57,140 --> 00:20:05,140
The root cause of this vulnerability was Atlassian using the Pulse Secure Virtual Traffic Manager product,

161
00:20:05,140 --> 00:20:08,140
which should not be confused with their VPN.

162
00:20:08,140 --> 00:20:17,140
We also already saw that Netlify was vulnerable and also needless to say Imperva's WAF was once again vulnerable.

163
00:20:17,140 --> 00:20:21,140
They haven't patched it yet, but hopefully they will have by the time you watch this.

164
00:20:21,140 --> 00:20:27,140
And I believe it works on some other targets as well, but those haven't been announced at the time of recording.

165
00:20:28,140 --> 00:20:41,140
So while they were waiting for Pulse Secure's fix, Atlassian decided to try out a few hotfix and run into a few more examples of what can go wrong when you're trying to patch these issues.

166
00:20:41,140 --> 00:20:45,140
The first issue was they were filtering header values, but not filtering header names.

167
00:20:45,140 --> 00:20:53,140
Just putting new lines in header names isn't directly useful, but you can also put colons in header names,

168
00:20:53,140 --> 00:21:00,140
resulting in a valid HTTP one of requests going to the back end and letting you successfully exploit.

169
00:21:00,140 --> 00:21:07,140
Another issue was that although they were filtering header names and values at this point, they weren't filtering pseudo headers.

170
00:21:07,140 --> 00:21:11,140
So you could even put new lines in the method of the request.

171
00:21:12,140 --> 00:21:22,140
And once again, this will let you cause desynchronization on the back end, provided you think about the format of the H1 request that you'll be generating.

172
00:21:22,140 --> 00:21:28,140
So here you have to put a valid request line before injecting your new line or the attack will fail.

173
00:21:29,140 --> 00:21:34,140
Finally, there was one last error, which is a classic one really.

174
00:21:34,140 --> 00:21:43,140
In the path pseudo header only, they were blocking the sequence slash R slash N, but they weren't blocking slash N by itself.

175
00:21:43,140 --> 00:21:50,140
And on almost all servers, you can use slash N by itself to cause desynchronization.

176
00:21:50,140 --> 00:22:01,140
So in summary, we've just seen a range of techniques that you can use to exploit H2 downgrades and achieve request smuggling, all demonstrated on live targets.

177
00:22:01,140 --> 00:22:08,140
Now I'm going to take a look at something less flashy and less obvious, but still really quite dangerous.

178
00:22:08,140 --> 00:22:21,140
When you find a request smuggling vulnerability, the possible attacks are affected by how the front end decides whether to reuse an existing connection to the back end or whether to create a new one.

179
00:22:21,140 --> 00:22:29,140
In a normal scenario, this is just done using a pool of connections and you can exploit other people with no issues.

180
00:22:29,140 --> 00:22:37,140
But sometimes you'll find you've got requests smuggling, but you can only poison the response to requests coming from your own IP.

181
00:22:37,140 --> 00:22:47,140
When you see that, that's because the front end has decided it's going to establish one connection to the back end per client IP that talks to it.

182
00:22:47,140 --> 00:22:55,140
This scenario makes direct cross user attacks less plausible, but it's still completely exploitable via cache poisoning.

183
00:22:55,140 --> 00:22:58,140
And it's easy to leak internal headers of your own requests.

184
00:22:58,140 --> 00:23:02,140
And so life is a bit trickier, but it's not that bad.

185
00:23:02,140 --> 00:23:08,140
And you can have a similar thing happening on a connection basis instead of a per IP basis.

186
00:23:08,140 --> 00:23:16,140
The most extreme scenario is when the front end just refuses to ever reuse connections with the back end.

187
00:23:16,140 --> 00:23:21,140
And this is what I encountered on Bitbucket.

188
00:23:21,140 --> 00:23:24,140
So I'm going to show you how to prove this even exists.

189
00:23:24,140 --> 00:23:29,140
And I'm going to share some new exploit paths that make it a lot more exploitable.

190
00:23:31,140 --> 00:23:34,140
Let's visualize what's happening here.

191
00:23:34,140 --> 00:23:39,140
In this example here, we've successfully smuggled 1.5 requests to the back end.

192
00:23:39,140 --> 00:23:44,140
But the front end is only sending one request down each socket.

193
00:23:44,140 --> 00:23:48,140
And that means that although we're poisoning a socket, the poison socket is being discarded.

194
00:23:48,140 --> 00:23:54,140
And any follow up requests, regardless of whether they're sent by the attacker or the victim,

195
00:23:54,140 --> 00:23:59,140
are going down a different socket and thereby being unaffected.

196
00:23:59,140 --> 00:24:03,140
You'll find this configuration happens naturally sometimes.

197
00:24:03,140 --> 00:24:13,140
And also Amazon's HDB Decent Guardian deliberately triggers this behavior whenever it sees a suspicious looking request.

198
00:24:13,140 --> 00:24:20,140
It's worth noting that Decent Guardian Spectacl clearly failed to use any mitigations against HDB2 powered attacks.

199
00:24:20,140 --> 00:24:23,140
But I imagine they'll be fixing that at some point.

200
00:24:23,140 --> 00:24:27,140
So when we encounter this, it causes numerous practical problems.

201
00:24:27,140 --> 00:24:31,140
And I'm going to show how to tackle some of them.

202
00:24:31,140 --> 00:24:40,140
The first problem is that although the normal request smuggling detection technique that's based on timeouts works absolutely fine.

203
00:24:40,140 --> 00:24:44,140
And we'll flag the systems as being vulnerable.

204
00:24:44,140 --> 00:24:54,140
The normal confirmation technique of sending a bunch of requests and then observing whether an initial requests affects a later one will fail.

205
00:24:54,140 --> 00:24:59,140
So when you fail to confirm it, it's very easy to mistake it for a false positive.

206
00:24:59,140 --> 00:25:08,140
With the understanding that I've just given you of how this is working, you might think it's actually quite easy to detect.

207
00:25:08,140 --> 00:25:12,140
Just smuggle a request and see if you get two responses.

208
00:25:12,140 --> 00:25:25,140
However, the response here doesn't actually show that this target is vulnerable because as we saw way back at the start, this is just how HDB1 keep alive works.

209
00:25:25,140 --> 00:25:29,140
There's no evidence this is vulnerable because we can't answer the question.

210
00:25:29,140 --> 00:25:33,140
Does the front end think it's sending us one response or two?

211
00:25:33,140 --> 00:25:38,140
HDB2 neatly fixes this problem for us.

212
00:25:38,140 --> 00:25:52,140
If you see HDB1 headers coming back in the body of HDB2 request, that clearly shows that you've successfully achieved requests model.

213
00:25:52,140 --> 00:25:56,140
The second problem is often even worse.

214
00:25:56,140 --> 00:26:09,140
This problem happens because a bunch of front end servers look at the content length that's coming back towards them from the back end in order to know how many bytes they should read and pass on to the end user.

215
00:26:09,140 --> 00:26:23,140
So this means you'll be able to trigger two responses from the back end, but you'll only ever see the first response, which is the less interesting one, making this extremely difficult to detect and exploit.

216
00:26:23,140 --> 00:26:27,140
And this was the scenario that occurred on Bitbucket.

217
00:26:27,140 --> 00:26:34,140
I didn't know this was what was happening and I certainly didn't have any particular solution in mind for this.

218
00:26:34,140 --> 00:26:48,140
But with research, if you spend long enough on a target, you can basically achieve a kind of brute force attack whereby you find the solution without having any idea what you're doing simply by spending a lot of time on it.

219
00:26:48,140 --> 00:26:50,140
And this is what happened to me.

220
00:26:50,140 --> 00:26:59,140
I was dealing with a particularly large response on Bitbucket and the file was so large that it was making Burp's UI lag slightly.

221
00:26:59,140 --> 00:27:02,140
And eventually this lag began to annoy me.

222
00:27:02,140 --> 00:27:03,140
So I thought, you know what?

223
00:27:03,140 --> 00:27:06,140
I don't even care about the response body.

224
00:27:06,140 --> 00:27:09,140
All I'm looking at is the response headers.

225
00:27:09,140 --> 00:27:17,140
So why don't I just change the method from post ahead and thereby only ask the back end to send me the request headers?

226
00:27:17,140 --> 00:27:25,140
As soon as I did this, I saw that I was actually getting multiple responses from the back end in one go.

227
00:27:25,140 --> 00:27:34,140
This is because the head method only asks for the headers, but the back end server still decided to send the content left header on the front end.

228
00:27:34,140 --> 00:27:35,140
And so it's all okay.

229
00:27:35,140 --> 00:27:41,140
I'd better read that many bites and ended up over reading into the next response, which was also intended for me.

230
00:27:41,140 --> 00:27:45,140
Thereby showing me both responses in one go.

231
00:27:45,140 --> 00:27:58,140
So through this completely accidental discovery, I found out a way that you can a technique you can use if you find blind request tunneling in order to make it non blind.

232
00:27:58,140 --> 00:27:59,140
It won't work on every service.

233
00:27:59,140 --> 00:28:06,140
Some front end service will have special casing for the head method, but it does work a decent proportion of the time.

234
00:28:06,140 --> 00:28:12,140
So let's say you found your request tunneling vulnerability.

235
00:28:12,140 --> 00:28:13,140
Maybe it's blind.

236
00:28:13,140 --> 00:28:14,140
Maybe it isn't.

237
00:28:14,140 --> 00:28:16,140
How are you going to exploit it?

238
00:28:16,140 --> 00:28:22,140
Well, because of the reconnection reuse behavior, we can't directly attack other users.

239
00:28:22,140 --> 00:28:27,140
But one thing you can do is take advantage of internal headers.

240
00:28:27,140 --> 00:28:38,140
Front ends often put secret headers on every request that are trusted by the back end server for critical functions like telling who the user is logged in as.

241
00:28:38,140 --> 00:28:43,140
And some of these are standards, so you can just guess the value of them.

242
00:28:43,140 --> 00:28:52,140
And I've just released an update for for prevent minor that supports nest mining the values of headers on internal requests like this.

243
00:28:52,140 --> 00:28:55,140
But often these will have custom names.

244
00:28:55,140 --> 00:28:59,140
And so you really need a way to find out what they're called in order to exploit.

245
00:28:59,140 --> 00:29:06,140
Normal header leaking techniques don't work when the front end isn't reusing the connection with the back end.

246
00:29:06,140 --> 00:29:15,140
But if you can inject new lines and headers, you can cause a different type of desynchronization that lets you disclose header names.

247
00:29:15,140 --> 00:29:24,140
Normal desynchronization techniques result in the front end and the back end disagreeing about where the message body ends.

248
00:29:24,140 --> 00:29:34,140
But if you look at what we're doing here, we're smuggling an extra start of a body inside a header.

249
00:29:34,140 --> 00:29:39,140
So both the front end and the back end view this as being one request.

250
00:29:39,140 --> 00:29:45,140
Even if this is a blind request underline vulnerability, we'll still see this response coming back to us.

251
00:29:45,140 --> 00:29:53,140
The desynchronization is happening because the two servers disagree about where the body of the request starts.

252
00:29:53,140 --> 00:29:59,140
This means that when the front end tries to put some internal headers on the end of the headers.

253
00:29:59,140 --> 00:30:05,140
It actually puts those internal headers in the body of the request on the S parameter.

254
00:30:05,140 --> 00:30:18,140
And all you need is a single reflected parameter like the search parameter that I used on the Bitbucket on the WordPress back end for Bitbucket in order to leak a whole bunch of internal headers.

255
00:30:18,140 --> 00:30:33,140
I also found just by changing the path and method that I used, I could trigger different request routing rules, hit completely different back end servers and leak different internal headers, including some which contain secret keys.

256
00:30:33,140 --> 00:30:39,140
This was juicy enough for me to report this issue to Bitbucket as is.

257
00:30:39,140 --> 00:30:49,140
But if you'd like a more detailed case study of exploitation of internal headers, check out the new relic example from my HDB desync attacks presentation.

258
00:30:49,140 --> 00:30:56,140
So finally, if the stars are aligned, you might be able to use tunneling for cash poisoning.

259
00:30:56,140 --> 00:31:14,140
If the target has a cash and the head technique works and you can put new lines in headers, then what you'll find is you have a unique and extra powerful variety of cash poisoning at your disposal.

260
00:31:14,140 --> 00:31:21,140
What you can do is mix and match response headers and response bodies.

261
00:31:21,140 --> 00:31:28,140
Here I've chosen a simple for four response for the headers, which has the content type of text HTML.

262
00:31:28,140 --> 00:31:36,140
And then I've chosen a different response, which simply reflects some user input in the location header without HTML encoding it.

263
00:31:36,140 --> 00:31:40,140
Now that behavior by itself is completely secure.

264
00:31:40,140 --> 00:31:52,140
You don't need to HTML encode data that's going in the location header, but because I've spliced it together with this other response, I've effectively injected arbitrary malicious JavaScript.

265
00:31:52,140 --> 00:31:58,140
And using this for cash poisoning, I could take full control over, I believe, every page on Bitbucket's website.

266
00:31:58,140 --> 00:32:06,140
For this, I got 15K, which was triple their maximum bounty.

267
00:32:06,140 --> 00:32:11,140
Now I'm going to take you on a tour of a few HDB2 exploit primitives.

268
00:32:11,140 --> 00:32:16,140
These are all using HDB2 features to give you some kind of hold on the target.

269
00:32:16,140 --> 00:32:21,140
And they're all based on behaviors that I've observed on real systems.

270
00:32:21,140 --> 00:32:32,140
Firstly, in HDB1, duplicate headers can be used for all kinds of attacks, but there's no way of specifying a duplicate path or a duplicate method.

271
00:32:32,140 --> 00:32:36,140
But in HDB2, you can, thanks to pseudo-headers.

272
00:32:36,140 --> 00:32:43,140
And I've verified a bunch of servers let you specify duplicate pseudo-headers, and they vary in terms of which ones they give priority to.

273
00:32:43,140 --> 00:32:49,140
So this could potentially enable some quite powerful attacks if you're willing to put the time in.

274
00:32:49,140 --> 00:33:02,140
Another issue is that in H2, in the specification, as I understand it, both the authority pseudo-header and the host-header are allowed, and they're both optional.

275
00:33:02,140 --> 00:33:10,140
This sets the scene for a whole variety of host-header attacks, because they're both meant to specify the same thing.

276
00:33:10,140 --> 00:33:15,140
So host-header attacks can be expected to live on in HDB2.

277
00:33:15,140 --> 00:33:18,140
You just need to tweak how you launch them slightly.

278
00:33:18,140 --> 00:33:24,140
Another issue with H2 is that they've introduced this scheme pseudo-header.

279
00:33:24,140 --> 00:33:27,140
I don't think this has a direct equivalent in H1.

280
00:33:27,140 --> 00:33:31,140
It's basically a fresh new piece of attack surface.

281
00:33:31,140 --> 00:33:35,140
And the value of the scheme is meant to be HTTP or HTPS.

282
00:33:35,140 --> 00:33:38,140
But of course, we can put whatever value we like in there.

283
00:33:38,140 --> 00:33:43,140
And unsurprisingly, a bunch of servers fail to verify it.

284
00:33:43,140 --> 00:33:50,140
For example, Netlify use the scheme to build a URL without verifying this value.

285
00:33:50,140 --> 00:33:57,140
And so you can make them get extremely confused about which path and endpoint you're talking to by putting the full URL in there.

286
00:33:57,140 --> 00:34:06,140
On a different target, you could trigger server-side request forgery because they use the scheme to build a URL and then route the request to that URL.

287
00:34:06,140 --> 00:34:08,140
It's worth mentioning.

288
00:34:08,140 --> 00:34:16,140
All the attacks I've shown you up to this point, recall, only work on targets that are doing HTTP2 downgrading.

289
00:34:16,140 --> 00:34:21,140
But this exploit works even if they're speaking H2 end to end.

290
00:34:21,140 --> 00:34:30,140
Now, something else you can do in H2 that works when downgrading is happening is you can put colons in header names.

291
00:34:30,140 --> 00:34:40,140
Now, sometimes you can use this for request smuggling, provided the backend server doesn't mind having a trailing colon in the transfer encoding header.

292
00:34:40,140 --> 00:34:42,140
However, a bunch of times this won't work.

293
00:34:42,140 --> 00:34:49,140
So what I would suggest is falling back to using it for host header attacks because the host header is expected to have a colon in it.

294
00:34:49,140 --> 00:34:56,140
And a whole bunch of servers just ignore everything after the colon because they think that must be the port.

295
00:34:56,140 --> 00:35:03,140
Now, I did get request smuggling on one server using this transfer encoding colon based technique.

296
00:35:03,140 --> 00:35:10,140
And I got halfway through exploitation and then I got bored and decided to take a break and come back later.

297
00:35:10,140 --> 00:35:14,140
And when I came back, I found the vulnerability had disappeared.

298
00:35:14,140 --> 00:35:19,140
And the server banner was reporting that they'd updated their version of Apache.

299
00:35:19,140 --> 00:35:25,140
So I went looking to see if Apache had an advisory relating to H2 power request smuggling.

300
00:35:25,140 --> 00:35:30,140
And they didn't. There wasn't anything that could conceivably be this issue.

301
00:35:30,140 --> 00:35:35,140
So I thought maybe they'd accidentally fixed this vulnerability.

302
00:35:35,140 --> 00:35:44,140
And I figured, I know, I'll just install the version of Apache locally, replicate the vulnerability and get a CV issued for it.

303
00:35:44,140 --> 00:35:46,140
So I set it up.

304
00:35:46,140 --> 00:35:48,140
It took a while, unfortunately.

305
00:35:48,140 --> 00:35:52,140
But when I got there, I found I couldn't replicate the vulnerability.

306
00:35:52,140 --> 00:35:55,140
I'd have no idea what was going on there.

307
00:35:55,140 --> 00:35:58,140
But I thought, well, since I've installed it, I might as well just give it a bit more testing.

308
00:35:58,140 --> 00:36:05,140
And what I found was a zero day that works just as well on the latest version of Apache.

309
00:36:05,140 --> 00:36:17,140
If you take Apache 2 and you use mod proxy to do HTTP to downgrading, then you'll find that it lets you put spaces in the method pseudo.

310
00:36:17,140 --> 00:36:31,140
This effectively gives you a request line injection vulnerability and provided that your back end server is tolerant and ignores trailing junk in the request line, which some of them definitely do.

311
00:36:31,140 --> 00:36:40,140
Then you can use this to bypass front end rules trying to block access to certain folders or trying to trap you in certain folders on the back end.

312
00:36:40,140 --> 00:36:49,140
I reported to that that to Apache straight away and then it's going to be patched in the next version of Apache, which hasn't come out yet.

313
00:36:49,140 --> 00:36:55,140
So this is a zero day right now. But hopefully by the time that you're watching this, it will have been fixed.

314
00:36:55,140 --> 00:36:59,140
Finally, a few practicalities.

315
00:36:59,140 --> 00:37:03,140
HP one and H two are spoken on the same port.

316
00:37:03,140 --> 00:37:08,140
So a client needs to know which protocol it should be speaking.

317
00:37:08,140 --> 00:37:21,140
And what it does is it relies on the server advertising the fact that it supports H two via a field during the TLS handshake, which occurs before any HTTP messages are set.

318
00:37:21,140 --> 00:37:28,140
However, some servers do support H two, but they forget to advertise this fact to the client.

319
00:37:28,140 --> 00:37:38,140
And this results in no clients actually speaking H two with them by default and can result in you missing out on some really juicy attack surface.

320
00:37:38,140 --> 00:37:41,140
Fortunately, this is extremely easy to detect.

321
00:37:41,140 --> 00:37:44,140
You can even just use curl if you want to.

322
00:37:44,140 --> 00:37:49,140
I found one vulnerable server where they they had this feature.

323
00:37:49,140 --> 00:37:53,140
H two wasn't visible, but I could exploit H two power requests smuggling on them.

324
00:37:53,140 --> 00:38:02,140
But there was a catch there, which is that due to their connection reuse style, I could only exploit other people that were using HTTP two, which was nobody.

325
00:38:02,140 --> 00:38:05,140
So it wasn't actually much use.

326
00:38:05,140 --> 00:38:20,140
There's another potential trap, which is although H two is supposed to have amazing request encapsulation, you'll often find a couple of bits of behavior that kind of failed to follow that line of thought.

327
00:38:20,140 --> 00:38:31,140
One is sometimes you'll send a request and all subsequent requests on that connection will fail, even though the server hasn't actually explicitly closed the connection.

328
00:38:31,140 --> 00:38:39,140
Another issue is that sometimes servers will just apply different rules to the first request that they receive on any given connection.

329
00:38:39,140 --> 00:38:55,140
So I've made sure in both burp suite and my own turbo intruder, you can control both of these properties to try and understand what's going on here and take advantage of whichever way the server happens to be doing things that I would go on this as an interesting topic.

330
00:38:55,140 --> 00:39:00,140
And I'm definitely going to be doing more research on it myself pretty much immediately.

331
00:39:00,140 --> 00:39:05,140
The tooling situation for HP two is not amazing.

332
00:39:05,140 --> 00:39:14,140
I think that's why aside from it, Emily was research, I almost got away with waiting a year before starting to work on this topic.

333
00:39:14,140 --> 00:39:22,140
Firstly, but virtual H two libraries refused to send the kind of mouthful requests that we rely on for this research.

334
00:39:22,140 --> 00:39:32,140
And because H two is a binary protocol, you it's completely extremely difficult to use things like Nick can open SSL to do these attacks manually.

335
00:39:32,140 --> 00:39:35,140
But you do have a few options.

336
00:39:35,140 --> 00:39:42,140
You've got HP two smuggle, which is in those tools based on the Golang H two stack, which he's made some patches to.

337
00:39:42,140 --> 00:39:49,140
There's also the HP two library built into turbo intruder, which I coded from scratch using socket.

338
00:39:49,140 --> 00:39:53,140
So if you want something really powerful that you can hack up, I'd recommend trying that.

339
00:39:53,140 --> 00:40:01,140
That works as a burp extension, but it can also be used as a completely standalone thing via the command line or even hooked as a library.

340
00:40:01,140 --> 00:40:03,140
However, I coded it.

341
00:40:03,140 --> 00:40:05,140
It's not the most viable thing in the world.

342
00:40:05,140 --> 00:40:08,140
So you may just prefer to use burp suite.

343
00:40:08,140 --> 00:40:22,140
We've done an update release today to the repeater and to the extended API so that you can take advantage of burp suite H two stack and still send these mouthful requests, which other libraries won't let you set.

344
00:40:22,140 --> 00:40:30,140
Finally, as far as detection of these vulnerabilities goes, a simple variation of the standard timeout technique works fine.

345
00:40:30,140 --> 00:40:37,140
And that's been implemented in burp scanner and in HP request smuggler, my open source burp suite extension.

346
00:40:37,140 --> 00:40:42,140
So I've just released a major update which adds support for all these techniques.

347
00:40:42,140 --> 00:40:47,140
And it was that tool that I used to find all the case studies for this presentation.

348
00:40:47,140 --> 00:40:55,140
I've also added support for finding for finding request smuggling vulnerabilities using that head technique that I showed.

349
00:40:55,140 --> 00:41:06,140
So although it's a bit less reliable in terms of it may miss out on vulnerabilities, sometimes it has the advantage of it has virtually no false positives.

350
00:41:06,140 --> 00:41:14,140
As far as the fence goes, the single best option is simply to speak HP two end to end instead of doing downgrade.

351
00:41:14,140 --> 00:41:21,140
If you do that, then almost all the attacks mentioned in this presentation will be more or less impossible.

352
00:41:21,140 --> 00:41:29,140
As far as other points go, well, if you're a server vendor, try and enforce HP one limitations.

353
00:41:29,140 --> 00:41:36,140
Totally let people put spaces in the method, new lines and headers, colons and header names, etc.

354
00:41:36,140 --> 00:41:45,140
Finally, if you're a developer, I think over time you'll just need to drop assumptions that it was safe to make in the world of HP one.

355
00:41:45,140 --> 00:41:49,140
Don't assume that header values aren't multiline.

356
00:41:49,140 --> 00:42:02,140
So for example, that means that it's no longer safe to take a header value and put it in an SMTP header, which is separated by new lines because you might have just created a head of injection vulnerability.

357
00:42:02,140 --> 00:42:05,140
Whereas in HP one, that would have been completely safe.

358
00:42:05,140 --> 00:42:10,140
Also, please, everybody don't trust the scheme pseudo header.

359
00:42:10,140 --> 00:42:12,140
I honestly think including this was a mistake.

360
00:42:12,140 --> 00:42:19,140
You should just be looking at the scheme that they're actually using and not the scheme that they claim they're using.

361
00:42:19,140 --> 00:42:22,140
If you have to use this, just use a whitelist.

362
00:42:22,140 --> 00:42:24,140
It should be HP or HPS.

363
00:42:24,140 --> 00:42:27,140
Anything else is simply an attack.

364
00:42:27,140 --> 00:42:30,140
There's a lot of further reading available.

365
00:42:30,140 --> 00:42:33,140
I do check out the white paper for this research.

366
00:42:33,140 --> 00:42:38,140
It will cover all this techniques, but from a written perspective, so it might make more sense.

367
00:42:38,140 --> 00:42:41,140
Also, we will be releasing a bunch of online labs.

368
00:42:41,140 --> 00:42:43,140
These may be available right now.

369
00:42:43,140 --> 00:42:48,140
And on these, they're accessible free and will spin up vulnerable servers for you.

370
00:42:48,140 --> 00:42:56,140
So you can practice applying all these techniques on real systems and get real practical experience with them.

371
00:42:56,140 --> 00:42:59,140
You can also grab the tool from GitHub.

372
00:42:59,140 --> 00:43:03,140
Emile's HP2 research is also worth checking out.

373
00:43:03,140 --> 00:43:05,140
You can watch the video there.

374
00:43:05,140 --> 00:43:18,140
And there's an upcoming DEFCON presentation called Response Smuggling Pwning HP 1.1 Connections that I've heard a little bit about and sounds really quite promising from an exploitation point of view.

375
00:43:18,140 --> 00:43:21,140
And I think a lot of it will work against HP 2 as well.

376
00:43:21,140 --> 00:43:23,140
So do check that out.

377
00:43:23,140 --> 00:43:40,140
